1. Resampling of the SEM Wavefield
   During resampling, the refined sampling interval (`deltat_refine`) is selected
   from the sequence

       delta_t_DSM / 2^n,   n = 1, 2, 3, ...

   (i.e., `delta_t_DSM/2`, `delta_t_DSM/4`, `delta_t_DSM/8`, etc.).

   `deltat_refine` is chosen to be the closest value to the original SEM sampling
   interval (`deltat_SEM` in `read.f90`). This strategy ensures compatibility
   between the SEM and DSM data while minimizing resampling errors. By selecting
   `deltat_refine` as `delta_t_DSM / 2^n`, the first `nfreq_DSM` frequency
   components of the FFT of the resampled SEM seismograms can be used directly
   for convolution with the DSM FFT, eliminating the need for additional
   frequency interpolation.

2. In the final step of the IFFT, zeros are appended to the frequency spectrum
   (`ismooth = 8`) to generate seismograms with a smaller sampling interval.
   This zero-padding increases the temporal resolution of the output waveforms
   without changing the frequency content.

To do:

1. Implement linear convolution to eliminate wrap-around effects.

   The current implementation performs circular convolution, causing the late
   portions of the seismograms to wrap around to the beginning of the time
   series. This issue can be eliminated by zero-padding the signals before
   convolution.

   However, applying zero-padding in the time domain requires several
   computationally expensive steps:

     a. Read all DSM frequency-domain data.
     b. Perform an inverse FFT (IFFT).
     c. Append zeros to the time-domain seismograms.
     d. Recompute the FFT.

   Although doubling the signal length through zero-padding provides a simple
   solution, only the even-indexed FFT coefficients remain identical to the
   original FFT. The odd-indexed coefficients must be obtained by sinc
   interpolation of the original FFT values, since zero-padding in the time
   domain is equivalent to sinc interpolation in the frequency domain.
   Consequently, all DSM frequency components must be read to perform the
   interpolation, making the procedure both computationally expensive and
   memory intensive.
